  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "direct judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The request concerns source correspondence, not uncertainty about what the declarations mean. Independent inspection confirms the equation-to-matrix linkage and initial-data semantics, but does not establish the entire effective-domain correspondence. Adjudication therefore preserves a specific unresolved issue rather than accepting or rejecting by agreement.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, defines an equation-plus-initial-data problem. D001, D009 and D018 additionally specify an arbitrary state predicate and its role in classification."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The blind translation accurately reconstructs the disputed declaration semantics. Its concerns about arbitrary state domains and global coordinate scope survive comparison with the exact Lean evidence; no translation error explains them.",
      "primary_evidence": "The blind translation's hyperbolicity definition matches D001 and D015; its equation triple matches D018; its three conclusions match the elaborated target."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined. The recorded interpretation settles positive dimension, problem-data classification, equal states and the free interface value. It does not expressly identify arbitrary stored state predicates with the source equation's domain or fix the global hyperbolicity domain.",
      "primary_evidence": "The recorded Q9 interpretation lists those selected conventions. Printed pages 1, 3 and 5 do not specify the additional arbitrary-subset model used by D018."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined for the same independently checked correspondence gap. Neither a faithful-stronger verdict nor a definite mismatch follows merely from the presence of more general equation fields.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5 uses the general term hyperbolic equation; printed page 8 includes equations outside conservation form. Neither passage supplies the full domain model reconstructed from D001 and D018."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D001 unclear",
      "resolution": "Its declaration meaning is fully determined: every principal matrix at every real x and t and every state in the stored set must have a real eigenbasis. Eigenstructure witnesses may vary with all three arguments. The matrix criterion matches the source; its exact generalized domain remains unresolved.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001 universally quantifies x, t and state before the membership implication. D015 existentially quantifies real eigenvalues and a basis. Printed page 3 gives the complete independent real-eigenvector criterion for the constant system and its flux-Jacobian analogue."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D002 unclear",
      "resolution": "D002 is an arbitrary stored subset, not a predicate derived from coefficient behavior. Treating it as the equation's declared state domain is coherent, but the selected convention does not establish that identification. Changing it can materially change classification.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002 projects the first field; D018 accepts any Set of state vectors. For scalar A = 1 and zero forcing, a full state set admits left 0 and right 1, whereas the set {0} rejects that same initial profile through D009."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D003 unclear",
      "resolution": "D003 permits arbitrary forcing independent of the spectral test. The homogeneous displayed examples do not prove that the definition excludes forcing, so forcing alone is not a demonstrated counterexample. Equally, those examples do not establish correspondence for every arbitrary forcing function.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 and D018 impose no forcing condition; D005 subtracts that forcing. Equations (1.1), (1.8) and (1.9) are homogeneous, while Section 1.2.1 says hyperbolic equation without an explicit zero-forcing restriction."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D004 unclear",
      "resolution": "The principal matrix is exactly the coefficient multiplying the formal spatial derivative in the residual, and exactly the matrix tested for hyperbolicity. This linkage passes. Arbitrary dependence on x, t and state is explicit; its full source scope remains unestablished.",
      "primary_evidence": "D004 projects the second equation field, used identically in D005 and D001. Printed page 3 discusses state-dependent flux Jacobians; printed page 8 discusses variable coefficients."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D008 unclear",
      "resolution": "The existential classification itself matches the recorded equal-state convention. It does not hide a distinctness requirement or a solution witness. Its remaining uncertainty is inherited solely from the governing-equation and state-domain interpretation in D009.",
      "primary_evidence": "D008 is exactly an existential pair of states satisfying D009. Equation (1.11) provides the two constant branches, and Q9 explicitly admits their equal-state degeneration."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D009 unclear",
      "resolution": "The conjunction is completely understood. Hyperbolicity and the strict-half-line data match the defining components under the recorded interpretation. Both additional membership conditions are material unless the stored subset is established as the source equation's state domain.",
      "primary_evidence": "D009 conjoins D001, left membership, right membership and D016. Printed page 5 supplies equation hyperbolicity and the two branches but no separately parameterized admissibility set."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D013 unclear",
      "resolution": "This is a concrete equation-expression representation rather than an opaque hyperbolicity tag. It stores no solution and requires no regularity. That is compatible with the selected problem-data abstraction, but compatibility alone does not establish exact coverage of source equations.",
      "primary_evidence": "D013 is specified by constructor D018 and projections D002-D004. D005 fixes its equation expression. Printed page 5 defines the problem without an existence or regularity theorem."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D018 unclear",
      "resolution": "The constructor imposes no compatibility, openness, nonemptiness or coefficient regularity conditions. Empty sets do not yield false-positive Riemann classifications because state witnesses are then impossible. Nonempty arbitrary subsets still present the substantive domain-correspondence question.",
      "primary_evidence": "D018 has only three data arguments. D009 requires both side states to belong to its first argument. Printed pages 3 and 5 do not define an arbitrary-subset equation model."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C02 unclear",
      "resolution": "Real coordinates and Fin (m+1) → Real pass under the recorded positive-dimensional interpretation, including the scalar case. The equation structure's field types are understood. The unresolved portion is the mathematical role of its arbitrary state subset, not an indexing or scalar-field error.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 uses real vector states and explicitly includes one component. The target quantifies m : Nat and uses successor dimension; D018 supplies the disputed equation fields."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "No classical differentiability or solution-existence hypothesis has been inserted. State membership and global hyperbolicity are nevertheless substantive conditions. Their justification cannot be supplied merely by naming them admissibility and hyperbolicity.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target contains no solution field or derivative predicate. D001 tests all real coordinates, and D009 tests memberships. Printed page 5 distinguishes discontinuous solutions from classical PDE satisfaction."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "Positive dimensions and degenerate equal states are expressly selected. The broader coefficient and state-domain representation is not automatically faithful strength: arbitrary state restrictions can remove applicable data, while arbitrary coefficients can enlarge the represented equation class. The net correspondence remains unresolved.",
      "primary_evidence": "Q9 specifies the selected conventions. D018 allows arbitrary subsets and functions. Printed page 8 supports variable-medium extensions but does not specify every such triple."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C03 unclear",
      "resolution": "The initial-state witness order and the dependence of eigenbasis witnesses pass. The remaining quantifier issue is global space-time scope. The passage supplies no precise solution time interval, so neither a forward-time restriction nor equivalence with all-real-time hyperbolicity can be silently assumed.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.11) fixes data at t = 0. D001 quantifies every real t. D015's eigenbasis witnesses occur inside each matrix test, while the target's two constant-state witnesses precede all half-line tests."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "The translation faithfully exposes the membership conditions and the state-restricted hyperbolicity test. These could express legitimate equation-domain typing, but the exact identification is not established by the recorded convention. Retain this uncertainty.",
      "primary_evidence": "The translation's set S corresponds exactly to D002 and D018, and its L,R ∈ S conditions correspond to D009. Section 1.2.1 introduces no independent S."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "The translation has not exaggerated the generality: arbitrary forcing, principal functions and subsets really are allowed. Nonconservation form is supported by the source, but it does not by itself justify every additional domain choice. No definite stronger classification is warranted.",
      "primary_evidence": "D018's constructor has no further premises. Printed page 8 discusses variable coefficients and nonconservation form; the recorded interpretation does not specify arbitrary state predicates."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left implication lean_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The implication is established for the explicitly represented homogeneous constant-coefficient systems on the full real state space under the recorded interpretation. Extending it to every formal equation accepted by D001 and D009 requires the unresolved equation-domain correspondence.",
      "primary_evidence": "For constant A, full state set and zero forcing, D005 reduces to (1.1), D015 matches printed page 3, and D016 matches (1.11). D018 also admits substantially broader equation data."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left implication source_implies_lean unclear",
      "resolution": "The selected initial-data convention yields the branch and construction clauses, and the residual clause is algebraic. A full reverse correspondence still requires specifying how each source equation supplies the stored state domain and global principal-matrix condition.",
      "primary_evidence": "D005 gives a-b = 0 iff a = b componentwise. D010 and D017 construct all free-origin profiles. D001 and D009 introduce the unresolved global and membership conditions."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The accurate blind translation does not close the source-domain gap. Its formal equation expression and two-state data preserve the selected definition's core, but membership in the intended source equation class is not established for every translated triple.",
      "primary_evidence": "The blind mathematical definitions agree with D001, D005, D009 and D018. Printed page 5 provides the broad defining phrase without the exact generalized domain specification."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "The reverse implication remains unclear for the full translated domain. This is not an objection that the source must explicitly print every algebraic consequence; it is a missing correspondence for conditions that actually alter which equation-data pairs qualify.",
      "primary_evidence": "The full-set versus singleton scalar example changes classification through D009 despite identical principal and forcing functions. Equation (1.11) and Q9 do not identify this additional parameter with source equation identity."
    },
    {
      "item": "Nonvacuity, boundary cases and supplementary conclusions",
      "resolution": "Nonvacuity passes: dimension one, full state set, A = 1 and zero forcing admit both equal and unequal states. Empty state sets cannot produce Riemann witnesses. Evaluating any proposed half-line witnesses at -1 and 1 fixes them uniquely, so equal-side data cannot be relabeled as a jump. Arbitrary origin values preserve the selected interface convention. The residual identity adds no applicability condition and asserts no solution.",
      "primary_evidence": "D007-D010 and D016-D017 determine the classification and construction. D005 is ordinary vector subtraction. Printed page 5 supplies the strict branches and only qualified typical solution behavior."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, Lean preserves positive-dimensional real states, the governing equation and its actual principal matrix, the two strict-half-line conditions, equal-state degeneration, arbitrary interface value and absence of a solvability assertion. The homogeneous constant-coefficient full-state-space correspondence is established. For the entire target domain, however, arbitrary stored subsets control both hyperbolicity and admissibility without an established identification with the source equation's effective domain. The broad source phrase does not conclusively exclude arbitrary forcing, but also does not establish exact correspondence for every unrestricted coefficient triple. Therefore the full implication remains unclear."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, the data characterization, genuine-jump distinction and free-origin construction follow from the selected definition; the residual equivalence follows from its explicit algebraic definition. These are harmless definitional consequences rather than unsupported solution claims. The full reverse correspondence additionally requires a justified assignment of admissibleStates and identification of source hyperbolicity with D001's all-real-space-time test. Neither is fixed by the supplied source passage or recorded convention. Imposing them as extra assumptions would reduce applicability, not establish genuine strength."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "Whether admissibleStates is an authorized representation of the source equation's effective state domain, including arbitrary nonempty subsets, rather than an additional independent filter on permitted Riemann data.",
    "Whether the intended source equation class corresponds exactly to arbitrary principal and forcing functions tested for real diagonalizability at every real space-time point on that stored subset.",
    "The printed source's admission of equal left and right states remains ambiguous. Q9 resolves this for the present comparison only; it does not resolve the book's wording."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-effective-domain-correspondence",
      "evidence": "D018 accepts an arbitrary state predicate independently of the principal and forcing functions. D001 tests hyperbolicity only on that predicate, and D009 requires both constant states to satisfy it. A full state set and the singleton {0} give different classifications for scalar advection with left state 0 and right state 1. Section 1.2.1 and Q9 do not specify how this parameter corresponds to source equation identity.",
      "impact": "Both full implication directions remain unresolved. The example demonstrates material dependence, not a definitive mismatch: a declared equation domain could legitimately distinguish the two problems, but that interpretation must be established."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-equation-and-coordinate-scope",
      "evidence": "D001 requires spectral hyperbolicity for all real x and t; D018 permits arbitrary principal and forcing functions. Printed pages 3 and 8 support real eigenstructure, state-dependent Jacobians and variable coefficients, but do not determine the entire unrestricted equation model or its global coordinate domain.",
      "impact": "Acceptance cannot be obtained by silently adding representation conventions. Conversely, homogeneous examples alone do not justify a definite rejection of nonzero forcing."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "confirmed-problem-data-semantics",
      "evidence": "D005 uses the same principal field as D001. D006 and D014 pair an equation with initial data. D016 imposes exactly the two strict branches; D017 permits every origin value. The target has no solution field or differential regularity premise.",
      "impact": "The equation linkage, initial-data content, nonvacuity and absence of an unsupported solution theorem pass under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-qualified-degenerate-case",
      "evidence": "Printed page 5 describes a jump but writes no explicit state inequality in (1.11). Q9 admits equal states. D008 includes them and D007 separately requires unequal side states.",
      "impact": "This distinction is faithful under the recorded interpretation only and must not be attributed to an unambiguous printed assertion."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Independent inspection of the attached primary-source renderings and exact inline declaration evidence confirms the blind translation and the central problem-data classification. The disputed dependencies have definite Lean meanings; the unresolved question is their full correspondence to the selected source equation class. The recorded convention settles several important ambiguities but does not authorize silently resolving every additional effective-domain and coordinate-domain choice. Nonvacuous instances exist, the algebraic residual clause is harmless, and no solution theorem is asserted. Neither acceptance nor a definitive mismatch is established for the full domain, so undetermined is the classification consistent with both unclear implications. This adjudication does not rely on majority vote and does not claim independent hash recomputation."
}
